idps-chips-signin = Sign-in and launch
idps-chips-signin-hint = Identity, launch context and refresh.
idps-chips-patient = Patient data (patient/)
idps-chips-patient-hint = Confined to the one patient chosen at sign-in or passed by the EHR.
idps-chips-user = User data (user/)
idps-chips-user-hint = Everything the signed-in user may see.
idps-chips-system = System (system/)
idps-chips-system-hint = Backend access without a user (client_credentials).
idps-chips-other = Other scopes
idps-chips-other-hint = Custom scopes, granted only when requested exactly.
idps-effective = What this app can get
idps-effective-desc = Its allowed scopes, by whose data they reach. Struck-through scopes are not offered by this server's settings and are never granted.
idps-effective-none = No scopes are allowed: the app cannot get a token.

idps-invalid = { $scope } is not a valid scope: { $reason }
idps-syntax-characters = scopes are single words without spaces, quotes or backslashes.
idps-syntax-resource-shape = a resource scope looks like patient/Observation.rs.
idps-syntax-resource-type = the resource type must be * or a FHIR type name such as Observation (case sensitive).
idps-syntax-permissions = permissions are letters from c r u d s in that order (rs, cruds), or read / write / * in SMART v1.
idps-syntax-v1-query = filters such as ?category= need v2 permissions (.rs, not .read).
idps-syntax-query = the filter after ? must be name=value pairs joined by &.
idps-syntax-launch = launch context scopes look like launch/patient or launch/encounter.
idps-syntax-case = scopes are case sensitive (fhirUser, patient/…).
idps-redundant = { $scope } is already covered by { $by }.

idps-f-empty = No scopes are allowed: the app cannot get a token.
idps-f-system-public = { $scopes }: system/ scopes need a confidential app using client_credentials; a public app never gets them.
idps-f-system-no-cc = { $scopes }: system/ scopes are only granted through client_credentials (Backend service), which this app does not use.
idps-f-user-scopes-backend = { $scopes }: patient/ and user/ scopes need a signed-in user; a backend service never gets them.
idps-f-standalone-no-launch-patient = { $scopes }: a standalone app needs launch/patient so the user can choose the patient these scopes are confined to.
idps-f-ehr-no-launch = EHR launch is on but the launch scope is not allowed: EHR launches will be refused.
idps-f-patient-no-context = { $scopes }: patient/ scopes need a patient — allow launch/patient (standalone) or launch (EHR launch).
idps-f-fhiruser-no-openid = { $scopes }: these come in the ID token, which needs openid.
idps-f-online-offline = online_access and offline_access are both allowed: an app asking for both gets refresh tokens that outlive the session.
idps-f-v1-syntax = { $scopes }: written in SMART v1 syntax. v2 requests are covered, but v1 apps can use them only with “Also accept SMART v1 scopes”.
idps-f-realm-off = { $scopes }: this server's settings turn off { $detail }, so these are never granted.
idps-f-write-protect-reads = { $detail } lets anyone read without a token (write-protect): read scopes don't restrict reading there. Write scopes are enforced.
idps-f-no-resource = No FHIR data scopes: the app can sign the user in but cannot read or write data.
idps-f-launch-not-ehr = launch is allowed but EHR launch is not enabled for this app.
idps-feature-permission-patient = patient/ scopes
idps-feature-permission-user = user/ scopes
idps-feature-backend-services = Backend services
idps-feature-sso-openid-connect = OpenID Connect
idps-feature-offline-access = offline access
idps-feature-online-access = online access
idps-feature-launch-ehr = EHR launch
idps-feature-launch-standalone = standalone and EHR launch

idps-how-title = How SMART access works
idps-how-flow-label = The token's scopes are what the app requests, intersected with the scopes allowed here and with what the user approves.
idps-how-flow-requested = Requested
idps-how-flow-requested-sub = scopes the app asks for
idps-how-flow-allowed = Allowed here
idps-how-flow-allowed-sub = this app's scopes
idps-how-flow-consent = Approved
idps-how-flow-consent-sub = on the consent screen
idps-how-flow-token = Access token
idps-how-flow-token-sub = what the app gets
idps-how-scopes-t = Scopes — what kinds of data
idps-how-scopes-d = Set per app. patient/Observation.rs lets the app read and search Observations; patient/*.rs every type.
idps-how-access-t = Resource access — which people
idps-how-access-d = Set per user. Their own record (fhirUser) and the patients they may open decide whose data a patient/ scope can reach.
idps-how-grant = Scopes the app asks for but are not allowed here are dropped silently; the token never gets more than the app is allowed.
idps-how-patient = patient/ scopes are confined to one patient: the one the user picks at sign-in (launch/patient) or the one the EHR passes (launch). The patients a user can pick come from their Resource access.
idps-how-user = user/ scopes reach everything the signed-in user may see — for clinicians and staff.
idps-how-system = system/ scopes are for backend services with no user (client_credentials), e.g. bulk export.
idps-how-perms = Permissions: c create, r read, u update, d delete, s search. The older v1 spelling .read means rs, .write means cud, .* means cruds.
idps-how-example-t = Example:
idps-how-example = the app allows patient/*.rs and launch/patient; Alice's Resource access lists Patient/123 and Patient/456. At sign-in Alice picks one of the two, and the app can read only that patient's records.
idps-how-to-users = Set which patients each user can open: Users → Resource access
idps-how-to-clients = Set what kinds of data each app may ask for: Apps → Allowed scopes
